> 0x2819e0b0 in strcpy () from /lib/libc.so.5

     Most likely the existing value of objects[DEAD_KOP].oc_name is
a pointer to the string "dead kobold" and gcc has told the linker
to put string literals into a region of memory which gets marked
read-only when the program executes.  So attempting to write to it
will indeed trigger this sort of error.  If that's the case, then
using `gcc -traditional' instead of `gcc -ansi', as I recommended
before, should allow this code to work.  Among other things,
traditional mode arranges to put string literals into a modifiable
region of memory.  But this is poor practice and the code should
probably be changed to

    objects[DEAD_KOP].oc_name = "dead Kop";

instead of relying on being able to overwrite a string literal.

     If do you change the compiler mode, recompile *everything*
with the new setting instead of mixing and matching object files
made with different settings.


